  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...A warm front north lifts north through the \nregion tonight through Wednesday. Low pressure then lifts north \nthrough the region Wednesday.\n\nHigh pressure over New York and Pennsylvania pushes out to sea \ntonight. Meanwhile, Low pressure tracks along the Eastern \nSeaboard and passes out to sea. Behind this low, a warm front \nwill lift north through the region late tonight through \nWednesday morning.\n\nClouds will lower and thicken late tonight, and some patchy fog\nmay develop as low level moisture increases over the area \nbehind the warm front lifting through the region. Surface dew \npoints generally in the mid 60s will rise into the upper 60s and\nlow 70s late tonight and Wednesday morning. Increasing \nsoutherly flow on Wednesday will push dew points up into the low\nto mid 70s Wednesday afternoon. With highs in the low to mid \n80s, max heat index values will be in the upper 80s to low 90s.\n\nAnother weak area of low pressure will track north Wednesday \nafternoon, mainly west of the Appalachians and west of the I-95\ncorridor into eastern Pennsylvania late Wednesday.\n\nIn terms of precipitation, some showers will develop in the \nmorning with the passage of the warm front, and then another \nround of showers and scattered thunderstorms will develop in the\nafternoon. The primary concern is for heavy rain leading to \nflash flooding, but when it comes to a hot and humid airmass, a \nfew strong to severe thunderstorms cannot be ruled out. \n\nThe Weather Prediction Center has a Marginal Risk (1 out of 4) \nfor Excessive Rain on Wednesday. Rainfall amounts will be light \nin the morning. While overall basin averages will generally be \n1/4 to 1/2 inch of rain, there is the potential for training \nleading to localized rainfall totals in excess of 1 inch. With \nmost of that rain occurring in the afternoon, that creates the \npotential for flash flooding.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...For the later half of the week, we will have a \nhot, humid, and at times stormy pattern in place.\n\nExpect a prolonged period of southwesterly flow as our region \nwill be in between a Bermuda High well to our east, and a mid \nlevel long wave trough that will be slowly sliding east over the\nnorthern tier of the U.S./southern Canada. As such, it will be \nhot and humid for the later half of the week and weekend with \nmaximum temperatures each day ranging from the low 80s (Poconos)\nto the low 90s (metro). However, with dewpoints from 70F to \n75F, heat index values at or above 100 degrees are possible for \nmuch of the area. \n\nThe other aspect of this are the daily storm chances, primarily\nconfined to the afternoon and evening with a typical diurnal \npattern. Given the hot and humid conditions that will be in \nplace, we can't rule out the more common thunderstorm hazards of\ndamaging winds and heavy rain/flooding. If an embedded short \nwave trough crosses our region as the larger trough axis \napproaches, that could increase the risk, particularly for \nsevere threats, but trying to time that out at this point would \nbe very difficult, given that we are still seeing some \ndifferences between the models with the larger pattern.",
